1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,865
This is ohwow, our heads-up poker bot.

2
00:00:02,865 --> 00:00:07,368
In about two minutes, we will show you how it thinks.

3
00:00:08,168 --> 00:00:11,477
Every match is one bot against one other bot.

4
00:00:11,477 --> 00:00:13,683
Each player gets two private cards,

5
00:00:13,683 --> 00:00:16,624
five shared cards come out on the board,

6
00:00:16,624 --> 00:00:18,462
and the blinds keep rising.

7
00:00:18,462 --> 00:00:20,668
Every time it is our turn,

8
00:00:20,668 --> 00:00:23,242
the bot has two seconds to fold,

9
00:00:23,242 --> 00:00:24,345
call or raise.

10
00:00:25,145 --> 00:00:28,395
Before the tournament, we computed how often every starting

11
00:00:28,395 --> 00:00:30,201
hand beats every other one.

12
00:00:30,201 --> 00:00:34,535
That is a table of one hundred sixty nine by one hundred

13
00:00:34,535 --> 00:00:38,147
sixty nine hands. It tells the bot instantly how strong

14
00:00:38,147 --> 00:00:39,592
its two cards are.

15
00:00:40,392 --> 00:00:44,023
When stacks get short, at thirteen big blinds or less,

16
00:00:44,023 --> 00:00:46,202
poker becomes all in or fold.

17
00:00:46,202 --> 00:00:49,107
We solved the Nash equilibrium for this offline,

18
00:00:49,107 --> 00:00:53,465
so the bot knows which hands to shove at every stack size.

19
00:00:53,465 --> 00:00:57,460
Then it adjusts if the opponent calls too much or too

20
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:57,823
little.

21
00:00:58,623 --> 00:01:02,006
After the flop, the bot runs thousands of simulations.

22
00:01:02,006 --> 00:01:05,765
It deals random cards to the opponent and finishes the

23
00:01:05,765 --> 00:01:08,396
board, then counts how often it wins.

24
00:01:08,396 --> 00:01:10,275
That number is its equity.

25
00:01:10,275 --> 00:01:14,410
Here, ace king on a king high board wins about ninety

26
00:01:14,410 --> 00:01:16,289
one percent of the time.

27
00:01:17,089 --> 00:01:19,665
But opponents do not hold random cards.

28
00:01:19,665 --> 00:01:23,344
When they bet, they are less likely to have nothing.

29
00:01:23,344 --> 00:01:27,390
So every simulated hand is weighted by how well it fits

30
00:01:27,390 --> 00:01:28,862
what the opponent did.

31
00:01:29,662 --> 00:01:31,813
Then the bot scores every option:

32
00:01:31,813 --> 00:01:33,964
check, a third of the pot,

33
00:01:33,964 --> 00:01:35,757
two thirds, the full pot,

34
00:01:35,757 --> 00:01:40,059
or an overbet. Each score is what we win when they fold,

35
00:01:40,059 --> 00:01:43,286
plus what we win or lose when they call.

36
00:01:43,286 --> 00:01:45,079
It picks the highest one.

37
00:01:45,879 --> 00:01:49,716
The key is that it learns each opponent during the match.

38
00:01:49,716 --> 00:01:53,203
It tracks how often they fold to each bet size,

39
00:01:53,203 --> 00:01:57,040
how often they bluff, and what their own bet sizes mean.

40
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:00,179
If a bot only bets big with strong hands,

41
00:02:00,179 --> 00:02:02,621
ours notices, and stops paying it off.

42
00:02:03,421 --> 00:02:07,480
It also defends itself. If an opponent re-raises us too

43
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:10,322
often, or raises our bets too often,

44
00:02:10,322 --> 00:02:13,163
the bot stops folding and fights back.

45
00:02:13,963 --> 00:02:18,006
It plays seven deuce aggressively for the side prize.

46
00:02:18,006 --> 00:02:22,497
It never crashes, because every decision has a safe fallback.

47
00:02:22,497 --> 00:02:25,641
And it decides in about fifteen milliseconds.

48
00:02:26,441 --> 00:02:29,405
We tested it like the real tournament.

49
00:02:29,405 --> 00:02:33,639
Duplicate matches, with the same cards and the seats swapped,

50
00:02:33,639 --> 00:02:36,180
against more than forty practice bots,

51
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:38,297
inside the official Docker sandbox.

52
00:02:38,297 --> 00:02:41,261
We also simulated thousands of whole tournaments.

53
00:02:41,261 --> 00:02:45,495
It wins about eighty percent of its matches against the

54
00:02:45,495 --> 00:02:46,342
practice field.

55
00:02:47,142 --> 00:02:51,553
Solid math as the base, and opponent learning on top.

56
00:02:51,553 --> 00:02:52,876
That is ohwow.
